  "title": "Erdős Problem #413",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $\\omega(n)$ count the number of distinct primes dividing $n$. Are there infinitely many $n$ such that, for all $m<n$, we have $m+\\omega(m) \\leq n$?\nCan one show that there exists an $\\epsilon>0$ such that there are infinitely many $n$ where $m+\\epsilon \\omega(m)\\leq n$ for all $m<n$?",
